#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111878 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to rewrite $\\log_3(4) - \\log_3(6)$ as a single logarithm. We can do this using the **quotient rule**. \n\nThe quotient rule is very similar to the product rule, except that this time we're dealing with the difference of two logarithms rather than the sum. \n\nThe quotient rule states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n} (a) - \\log_{\\color{blue}n} (b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(\\dfrac a b\\right) .\n$$\n\nSo, we can use this rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}3}(4) - \\log_{\\color{blue}3}(6)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(4)-{log}_{3}⁡(6) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{4}{6}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." :tutorial/id #uuid "3ed13d12-2b3d-5700-8a30-aa21719ebea3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2784 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
